     Presumably the terminal application allows you to launch
a program with a command line, so yes, that's what you need.

recover 501playername

from the directory (aka folder) where nethack left all those
files should do it.  If recover itself is located in a different
directory, you'll need to precede it with a path.  Or run it in
its directory and tell it where to find the nethack directory:
recover -d somewhere 501playername

     Depending upon why nethack crashed in the first place,
the resulting save file may or may not work.  If any of those
level files already have corrupted data, you're liable to get
another crash.



